## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals a clear pattern in the Bidens' reported taxable income: it was over $11 million in 2017, dropped to over $4.5 million in 2018, and fell further to approximately $945,000 in 2019. This shows a significant and consistent decline in their combined income over the two most recent tax years available in the evidence, directly contradicting any notion of a recent increase. While 2016 income data is absent, the trajectory from 2017 to 2019 is unambiguously downward.

The evidence is explicit and covers the relevant years leading up to the claim's date. No further information is needed to assess the claim of a "recent" increase, as the available data definitively shows the opposite trend.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check is **refuted** because the reported taxable income for Joe and Jill Biden shows a significant decline in the two years immediately preceding the claim, falling from [more than $11 million in 2017](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-releases-2019-tax-returns-ahead-first-debate-n1241387) to [more than $4.5 million in 2018](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-releases-2019-tax-returns-ahead-first-debate-n1241387) and then to [about $945,000 in 2019](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-releases-2019-tax-returns-ahead-first-debate-n1241387). This trend, [largely driven by a spike and subsequent drop in book deal income](https://www.denverpost.com/2020/09/29/biden-releases-2019-taxes/), directly contradicts any claim of a recent increase.